<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arturo Toscanini</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1867">1867</a>–<a href="/years/1957">1957</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Arturo Toscanini was an Italian conductor, born in Parma in 1867, who began as a cellist and first conducted in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a> in 1886, leading Aida from memory at the age of 19. He conducted the world premieres of Leoncavallo's Pagliacci and Puccini's La bohème, was principal conductor and later music director of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-scala">La Scala</a>, and led the Metropolitan Opera and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> Philharmonic. An opponent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Mussolini</a>, he refused to conduct the Fascist anthem <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giovinezza">Giovinezza</a> and was beaten by Blackshirts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a> in 1931. From 1937 to 1954 he was the first music director of the NBC Symphony Orchestra, whose broadcasts and recordings reached a mass audience. He died in New York in 1957.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/arturo-toscanini.jpg" alt="Arturo Toscanini" width="330" height="445" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown (Mondadori Publishers) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arturo_Toscanini_c1930.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The conductor <b>Arturo Toscanini</b>, who had stood as a Fascist candidate in 1919, refused to conduct it in Milan in 1922 and later at Bayreuth.</blockquote><a href="/songs/giovinezza">Giovinezza</a></li><li><blockquote>…1943 the BBC's Salute to the Red Army brought a mass performance to the Royal Albert Hall, and <b>Arturo Toscanini</b> insisted on including his orchestration of the song in the 1944 film Hymn of the Nations; during the McCarthy era it was cut from re-released copies until a…</blockquote><a href="/songs/the-internationale">The Internationale</a></li><li><blockquote>A month later, when he was reburied beside his wife at the Casa di Riposo, the young <b>Arturo Toscanini</b> conducted a choir of 820 singers in it.</blockquote><a href="/songs/va-pensiero">Va, pensiero (Chorus of the Hebrew Slaves)</a></li><li><blockquote>It became one of the world's leading opera houses, closely linked with Verdi, Puccini and <b>Toscanini</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-3">3 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Conducted by the young <b>Arturo Toscanini</b> at the Teatro Regio, Puccini's opera of struggling artists in Paris received a cool critical welcome.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-1">1 February in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Parma, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.8,10.33&amp;year=1925" aria-label="Parma on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4796 581 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Arturo Toscanini"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5246 851h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5246 851h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=44.8,10.33&amp;year=1925">What was here in 1925? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
